1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,472
This horror demo promises no jumpscares.
Its build quietly makes the same kind of promise about everything else.

2
00:00:06,722 --> 00:00:10,608
There is exactly one non-engine assembly in it.
One.

3
00:00:14,458 --> 00:00:15,966
Let's do the game first.

4
00:00:16,216 --> 00:00:24,046
The No Jumpscare Show. In its world the paranormal is ordinary,
and jumpscares are a lazy cliche everybody is tired of.

5
00:00:24,296 --> 00:00:32,361
So a girl called Illy signs up for a haunted house that advertises the opposite.
Real psychological horror, no cheap shots.

6
00:00:32,611 --> 00:00:36,732
Survive the night without getting scared
and you win ten thousand dollars.

7
00:00:41,982 --> 00:00:49,290
It's black and white. All of it. Not drained of colour afterwards,
built that way, with checkerboard floors and paper faces.

8
00:00:49,540 --> 00:00:58,023
And the monsters have names, and they talk to you. Thingy. Skelly Billy.
Manicus, who says hello and thanks you for coming to the show.

9
00:00:58,273 --> 00:01:04,614
That's the joke and the threat in one move.
Nothing leaps out. Things walk up and start a conversation.

10
00:01:04,864 --> 00:01:10,186
It went up yesterday. English only,
four reviews, and a full game with no date on it.

11
00:01:10,436 --> 00:01:11,892
Which leaves the build.

12
00:01:12,142 --> 00:01:16,628
Three hundred and thirty-nine megabytes.
Two hundred and sixty-two files.

13
00:01:16,878 --> 00:01:24,421
Unity, on the Mono backend. Mono matters here,
because it means the assembly list is readable instead of compiled away.

14
00:01:24,671 --> 00:01:31,770
That list is where every one of these episodes has found its story.
Who they bought from. What they forgot to take out.

15
00:01:32,020 --> 00:01:34,181
So here is this one, in full.

16
00:01:34,431 --> 00:01:39,231
Assembly-CSharp. Their own code,
compiled. And then nothing.

17
00:01:39,481 --> 00:01:48,800
No DOTween. No Odin. No Steamworks wrapper, no asset-store shaders,
no text animator, no save system, no pathfinding library.

18
00:01:49,050 --> 00:01:53,641
A haunted-house demo that went up the same
day carries more than forty of those.

19
00:01:53,891 --> 00:01:57,541
There's no StreamingAssets folder either.
No Plugins folder.

20
00:01:57,791 --> 00:02:06,692
And no folder called BackUpThisFolder_ButDontShipItWithYourGame.
Unity writes that name for you, and three demos I've opened this month shipped it anyway.

21
00:02:06,942 --> 00:02:10,462
The hundred and eight DLLs
in here are all Unity's own.

22
00:02:10,712 --> 00:02:14,833
So if three hundred megabytes didn't go into packages,
where did it go?

23
00:02:15,083 --> 00:02:21,711
Into the rooms. level10 on its own is seventy-two megabytes.
One scene, twenty-one percent of the game.

24
00:02:21,961 --> 00:02:33,318
Ninety-eight megabytes of resource files and sixty-two of resS beside it.
Audio and textures, sitting exactly where Unity leaves them when nobody has built a pipeline.

25
00:02:33,568 --> 00:02:36,513
It is the plainest build I
have opened on this channel.

26
00:02:36,763 --> 00:02:40,884
Reception is four reviews and one day.
There's nothing to read there yet.

27
00:02:41,134 --> 00:02:47,344
So here's the honest read.
This is what it looks like when a small team writes the thing and doesn't go shopping.

28
00:02:47,594 --> 00:02:56,965
That is not automatically better, and I want to be clear about it.
Bought packages are how small teams ship at all. I've said exactly that about six demos in a row.

29
00:02:57,215 --> 00:03:03,425
But after fifteen builds where the story was what somebody left in,
a list with nothing on it is worth stopping on.

30
00:03:03,675 --> 00:03:05,941
Here's the whole teardown on one card.

31
00:03:06,191 --> 00:03:15,223
Unity on Mono, three hundred and thirty-nine megabytes, two hundred and sixty-two files,
and one assembly that isn't the engine's — which is theirs.

32
00:03:15,473 --> 00:03:23,434
Worth a dip. It's free, it's genuinely strange,
and a monster that introduces itself politely turns out to be worse than one that jumps.

33
00:03:23,684 --> 00:03:32,663
If you ship in Unity, open your own Managed folder and count what's in it.
There's no prize for a short list. But there is a lot of information in yours.

34
00:03:32,913 --> 00:03:42,284
That's my read from the outside. If you worked on this and I got something wrong,
say so and I'll pin the correction. Next time, another demo, another file list. This is Demo Dip.
